## 5. Architectural Principles

These six principles are load-bearing; every design decision below traces back to one of them.

**P1 — Deterministic risk core, probabilistic reasoning shell.**
LLM agents plan, research, and score. They never hold a signing key, never call a DEX router directly, and never bypass SafetyCaps. This is the same pattern independently converged on across current agentic-trading builds: the model is the *architect* of the strategy and risk logic (design-time), not necessarily the *executor* of every individual trade (run-time) — ordinary, auditable, fast code enforces the boundary at run-time. In this system a live LLM *is* in the reasoning loop for market judgment (rug screening nuance, narrative risk, entry timing), but it is architecturally incapable of being the last step before a signature.

**P2 — Least-privilege custody via Agentic Wallets.**
TON Tech's Agentic Wallets standard gives each agent a dedicated, non-custodial, balance-scoped on-chain wallet funded by the operator's main wallet — the agent can transact only within its allocated balance, with no private-key handoff. This is used as the custody primitive instead of a single shared hot wallet: a compromised or misbehaving strategy can only lose what it was allocated, never the treasury.

**P3 — Containment, not filtering, against prompt injection.**
Since prompt injection is treated industry-wide in 2026 as unsolved at the model layer, this system does not attempt to guarantee that injected content will be detected. Instead every tool an agent can call is scoped to the minimum privilege it needs, every high-impact action requires a non-LLM check, and a five-layer sanitization pipeline (§10.4) reduces — but does not solely rely on reducing — the chance an injection lands.

**P4 — HITL intensity scales with capital at risk**, not with system complexity. A ฿50 scan-and-log action needs no approval; a trade above the auto-approve ceiling always needs a Telegram tap, regardless of how confident the agent is.

**P5 — Everything is journaled.** Every LLM call, every tool call, every cap check, every rejection, and every fill is written to an append-only decision journal before the next step proceeds. If it isn't journaled, it didn't happen, as far as the system is concerned for post-mortems.

**P6 — Fail closed.** Timeouts, missing RPC data, ambiguous rug scores, and unhandled exceptions all resolve to *do not trade* / *exit if already in position*, never to a default "proceed."

