## 7. Orchestration Layer (LangGraph Deep Agents)

### 7.1 Framework choice & rationale

| Framework | Fit for this system | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **LangGraph + Deep Agents** | Graph-based state machine with checkpointing, native human-in-the-loop interrupts (`interrupt_before`), and a Deep Agents layer providing todo-based planning, a virtual file system for agent memory, and sub-agents callable as ordinary tools. Any compiled sub-graph can be wrapped as a tool for a parent supervisor. | **Selected** — already the direction of the existing GRAM bot codebase |
| CrewAI | Fastest to prototype (~20 lines for a crew), but coarser state control; teams commonly migrate off it once edge cases need fine-grained conditional routing — exactly this system's requirement (risk gates, HITL branches, exit interrupts). | Rejected for orchestration; fine for throwaway prototyping |
| AutoGen / AG2 | Strong for open-ended multi-agent conversation; weaker on the compiled, resumable state-machine guarantees this system needs for auditability. | Rejected |
| Raw ReAct single-agent loop | Cannot hold multi-step plans, loses context on long scans, no native HITL interrupt. | Rejected |

**Cost note:** a supervisor-of-specialists pattern costs roughly 3× a single mega-agent call because every routing decision is itself an LLM turn. This system accepts that cost only where it buys a real safety or quality benefit (rug analysis, strategy formation) and uses a **cheaper/faster model tier for high-frequency, low-ambiguity tool-calling agents** (market scanning, formatting) and a **frontier reasoning tier** only for the supervisor and the risk analyst.

### 7.2 Orchestrator graph topology

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    START([Scheduler Tick / Telegram Command]) --> SUP{Supervisor}
    SUP -->|scan market| MS[Market Scanner subgraph]
    MS --> SUP
    SUP -->|candidate jetton| RA[Rug / Risk Analyst subgraph]
    RA -->|risk score + verdict| SUP
    SUP --> GATE{Risk Gate}
    GATE -->|reject| LOG1[Log & Discard]
    GATE -->|pass| SA[Strategy Agent<br/>sizing + entry/exit plan]
    SA --> SUP
    SUP -->|proposed trade ticket| CAPCHK{SafetyCaps<br/>Deterministic Check}
    CAPCHK -->|fail| LOG2[Log & Discard]
    CAPCHK -->|pass, below auto-limit| EA[Execution Agent]
    CAPCHK -->|pass, above auto-limit| HITL[Telegram Approval Request]
    HITL -->|approved| EA
    HITL -->|denied / timeout| LOG3[Log & Discard]
    EA --> MCPX[("@ton/mcp Agentic Wallet")]
    MCPX --> CHAIN[(TON Blockchain)]
    EA --> JRNX[(Decision Journal)]
    EA --> MON[Position Monitor]
    MON -->|exit condition met| EXITP[Exit Policy Engine]
    EXITP --> EA
```

This is a **plan-then-execute** topology with an explicit risk gate and an explicit cap check as separate graph nodes — not folded into agent reasoning — so both are independently unit-testable and cannot be "reasoned around" by a persuasive prompt.

### 7.3 Agent roster

| Agent | Responsibility | Tools it may call | Model tier | Can it authorize a trade? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Supervisor** | Routes between specialists, holds top-level plan (todo list via Deep Agents planning tool), decides when a cycle is complete | Sub-agents as tools, journal writer | Frontier | No |
| **Market Scanner** | Pulls trending/new pools from DEX Screener-style sources and on-chain indexers, filters by liquidity/age/volume thresholds | Read-only market-data tools | Cheap/fast | No |
| **Rug / Risk Analyst** | Scores a candidate jetton: LP-lock status, holder concentration, mint/blacklist authority, TVL trajectory, contract verification, creator wallet history | Read-only chain tools (`@ton/mcp` balance/jetton-info calls), rug-detection module (existing) | Frontier (nuanced judgment) | No — advisory score only |
| **Strategy Agent** | Given a passed candidate, proposes position size (within caps), entry method, stop-loss/take-profit/trailing parameters | Sizing calculators, exit-policy config reader | Frontier | No — proposes a *ticket*, not an authorization |
| **Execution Agent** | Converts an *authorized* ticket into a signed intent via `@ton/mcp`, submits, confirms | `@ton/mcp` transact tools only | Cheap/fast (deterministic tool-calling, minimal reasoning) | Executes only after SafetyCaps + (if required) HITL sign-off |
| **Position Monitor** | Polls open positions against exit-policy thresholds | Read-only price/chain tools | Cheap/fast, high frequency, largely non-LLM | No |
| **Postmortem / Journal Agent** | Periodically summarizes closed trades into human-readable Telegram digests | Journal reader | Cheap/fast | No |

### 7.4 State schema

The graph state is a single typed object threaded through every node — this is what gets checkpointed, journaled, and inspected during HITL pauses.

```python
class GramTradeState(TypedDict):
    cycle_id: str
    candidate: JettonCandidate | None
    risk_assessment: RiskAssessment | None      # from Rug/Risk Analyst
    proposed_ticket: TradeTicket | None          # from Strategy Agent
    cap_check_result: CapCheckResult | None      # from SafetyCaps — authoritative
    hitl_status: Literal["not_required", "pending", "approved", "denied", "timeout"]
    execution_result: ExecutionResult | None
    open_positions: list[Position]
    todo_plan: list[TodoItem]                    # Deep Agents planning surface
    journal_ref: str                              # pointer into append-only journal
```

`RiskAssessment`, `TradeTicket`, and `CapCheckResult` are separate types deliberately: a `TradeTicket` proposed by the Strategy Agent is *not itself authorization* — it only becomes actionable once wrapped in a `CapCheckResult` produced by non-LLM code.

### 7.5 Planning, memory, and sub-agent isolation

Following the Deep Agents pattern already in use, the Supervisor maintains a todo-style plan for each trading cycle and writes intermediate artifacts (candidate lists, risk notes, draft tickets) to a virtual file system rather than stuffing everything into a single growing context window. Each specialist sub-agent is a complete, independently-checkpointed sub-graph invoked as a tool by the Supervisor — this means the Rug/Risk Analyst can be upgraded, re-prompted, or swapped for a different model without touching the Execution Agent, and a failure or infinite loop in one specialist cannot corrupt another's state.

### 7.6 Human-in-the-loop interrupts

LangGraph's `interrupt_before` mechanism is used at exactly two points in the graph: **before** the Execution Agent node when a ticket exceeds the auto-approve ceiling, and **before** any action that would change SafetyCaps configuration itself (raising a cap requires explicit operator approval, never agent-proposed). The checkpointer persists full state at the interrupt, so approval can arrive minutes or hours later via Telegram without losing context, and a rejected or timed-out interrupt resumes into the "discard and log" branch, never into a default-proceed branch (per P6).

