## 9. SafetyCaps — Deterministic Risk & Safety Core

This is the layer that makes the rest of the system safe to leave running unattended. Every sub-section below corresponds to an existing or planned module; this document specifies the **contract** each module exposes to the orchestration layer, not its internal implementation.

### 9.1 Position & exposure caps

Deterministic, config-driven, and checked in plain Python with no LLM call in the critical path:

| Parameter | Example default | Enforced against |
|---|---|---|
| Max position size | % of strategy sub-wallet balance per trade | Every proposed ticket |
| Max exposure per token | % of sub-wallet balance | Sum of open positions in that jetton |
| Max concurrent open positions | integer count | Live position count |
| Max daily loss (circuit-breaker trigger) | % of sub-wallet balance in rolling 24h | Realized + unrealized PnL |
| Max single-trade slippage | basis points | Quoted vs. executed price |
| Min liquidity-depth ratio | trade size ≤ X% of pool TVL | Pool state at execution time |

Any proposed ticket that fails **any** check is rejected outright — checks are not weighted or averaged; this is a hard gate, not a scoring function.

### 9.2 Circuit breaker / kill switch

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Active
    Active --> Throttled: daily_loss > warn_threshold
    Throttled --> Active: daily_loss resets (UTC rollover)
    Throttled --> Halted: daily_loss > hard_threshold
    Active --> Halted: infra_anomaly (RPC failure streak, price feed divergence)
    Active --> Halted: manual_operator_halt (Telegram /halt)
    Halted --> Active: manual_operator_resume (Telegram /resume, explicit confirmation)
    Halted --> EmergencyExit: rug_signal on open position
    EmergencyExit --> Halted
```

- **Active**: normal operation, all agents may propose trades.
- **Throttled**: new position sizes are automatically reduced (e.g., halved) and the HITL auto-approve ceiling drops; existing positions continue to be monitored for exit.
- **Halted**: no new positions may open under any circumstances, including operator override via chat text — only an explicit `/resume` Telegram command with confirmation reactivates the system. Open positions are still monitored and can still be exited.
- **Emergency exit**: if a rug/risk re-assessment on an *already open* position crosses a critical threshold, the exit-policy engine is invoked directly regardless of circuit-breaker state — getting out of a bad position is never blocked by the halt.

### 9.3 Rug / honeypot detection module

The detection approach follows the two empirically-strongest signal families identified in TON-specific research: a **TVL-trajectory approach** (liquidity added/removed deltas, time-to-first-large-withdrawal, max-TVL-within-N-minutes-of-creation) and an **idle/activity approach** (transaction cadence, unique trader growth), combined — TVL-based features are more informative and stable by AUC across samples, while idle-based features achieve the strongest raw precision for the highest-confidence rejections. In addition to the on-chain ML signal, the module applies the standard rule-based checklist used across the DEX-security tooling ecosystem:

| Check | Signal |
|---|---|
| LP lock status | Locked/burned vs. freely withdrawable |
| Holder concentration | Top-10 wallet share of supply |
| Mint / blacklist authority | Whether the jetton owner retains mint or transfer-block functions |
| Contract verification | Source published and matches deployed code |
| Sell simulation | Small test-sell succeeds without excessive/asymmetric tax |
| Creator wallet history | Prior association with known rug addresses (behavioral, not just code-level — sophisticated operators write clean code but reuse compromised wallets) |
| Ticker-collision check (**TON/GRAM-specific**) | Confirms the jetton master address matches the known native-asset identity and is not a lookalike jetton exploiting the June 2026 Toncoin→GRAM rebrand naming confusion |

Output is a `RiskAssessment` with a numeric score and a categorical verdict (`pass` / `caution` / `reject`); only `pass` proceeds to the Strategy Agent automatically, `caution` requires HITL regardless of trade size, `reject` is auto-discarded.

### 9.4 Prompt-injection containment pipeline

Five independent layers, consistent with current defense-in-depth practice, applied to **every** piece of externally-sourced text before it enters an agent's context (token names/descriptions, social sentiment snippets, DEX pool metadata, any Telegram message content that gets relayed into agent context):

1. **Input filtering** — strip zero-width characters, unicode tricks, and known injection-pattern blocklist matches before the text reaches any model.
2. **Instruction delimiting** — externally-sourced content is always wrapped in structured, clearly-delimited tags distinct from system/operator instructions, so the model has a structural signal for "this is data, not instruction."
3. **Tool allow-listing per agent** — the Market Scanner and Rug/Risk Analyst, which touch the most untrusted external text, have *no* transact-capable tools available in their tool set at all — an injected instruction has nothing dangerous to invoke even if it "succeeds."
4. **Output validation** — structured-output schemas (Pydantic) reject any agent response that doesn't conform, catching injected attempts to smuggle instructions into what should be a numeric score or enum verdict.
5. **Architectural containment (the backstop)** — even a fully successful injection that convinces an agent to "recommend" an oversized or malicious trade still has to pass the non-LLM SafetyCaps gate and, above the auto-approve ceiling, an explicit human tap in Telegram. This layer is why layers 1–4 are defense-in-depth rather than the sole line of defense.

### 9.5 Exit policy engine

State machine per open position (diagram in §7.2 context; detailed below):

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Monitoring
    Monitoring --> TakeProfit: price >= tp_target
    Monitoring --> StopLoss: price <= sl_floor
    Monitoring --> TrailingActive: price >= trailing_trigger
    TrailingActive --> TrailingStop: price falls trailing_pct from local peak
    TrailingActive --> TakeProfit: price >= tp_target
    Monitoring --> TimeExit: hold_time >= max_hold_duration
    Monitoring --> EmergencyExit: rug_reassessment == critical
    TakeProfit --> Exited
    StopLoss --> Exited
    TrailingStop --> Exited
    TimeExit --> Exited
    EmergencyExit --> Exited
    Exited --> [*]
```

The exit policy is evaluated by the **Position Monitor** on a fast, largely non-LLM polling loop (price checks are arithmetic, not reasoning) — an LLM call is only invoked for `EmergencyExit` re-assessment, which is intentionally the one path that goes back through the Rug/Risk Analyst mid-position.

### 9.6 HITL escalation matrix

| Trade size (% of sub-wallet) | Risk verdict | Approval required |
|---|---|---|
| Below auto-approve floor | `pass` | None — auto-executes, logged |
| Below auto-approve floor | `caution` | Telegram approval, no timeout auto-deny... **times out to deny** (P6) |
| Above auto-approve ceiling | `pass` | Telegram approval required |
| Above auto-approve ceiling | `caution` | Telegram approval + explicit risk-note shown, longer review window |
| Any size | `reject` | Never surfaced for approval — auto-discarded |
| Cap-configuration change | n/a | Always requires operator-initiated action; never agent-proposed |

