## 11. Telegram Interface Layer

### 11.1 Command surface

| Command | Effect | Auth level |
|---|---|---|
| `/status` | Current circuit-breaker state, open positions, today's PnL | Operator |
| `/positions` | Detail view of open positions vs. exit policy | Operator |
| `/halt` | Immediate circuit-breaker → Halted | Operator |
| `/resume` | Halted → Active, requires confirmation tap | Operator |
| `/caps` | View current SafetyCaps parameters (read-only via chat) | Operator |
| `/setcap <param> <value>` | Propose a cap change | Operator, requires confirmation |
| `/digest` | On-demand postmortem summary | Operator |

### 11.2 Approval UX

HITL approval requests use Telegram inline-keyboard buttons (Approve / Deny / View Detail), not free-text parsing — this both improves usability on mobile and eliminates an entire class of prompt-injection surface where a maliciously-crafted "approval message" text could otherwise be misparsed. Each approval request includes: jetton identity + explorer link, risk verdict and score, proposed size as % of sub-wallet, quoted slippage, and which cap(s), if any, are near their ceiling.

### 11.3 Notification tiers

`info` (digest-batched), `action` (trade executed, position closed), `warning` (throttled state, cap near ceiling), `critical` (halted, emergency exit fired) — critical and action-required notifications are never batched.

